# Python Port Plan — Step 07 · The `Boukensha.run` DSL

## Purpose

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/07_the_run_dsl` to Python, producing
`week1_baseline/python/07_the_run_dsl` that is behaviourally equivalent:
one top-level entry point, `Boukensha.run(task:, ...) { tool ... }`, that
wires together `Config`/`Context`/`Registry`/a backend/`PromptBuilder`/
`Client`/`Logger`/`Agent` internally so callers only describe *what* to do.
A tiny `RunDSL` host object (`instance_eval`'d against the block) exposes
exactly one method, `tool`, deliberately keeping the DSL surface minimal.

This is the eighth entry in the `docs/plans/python_port/<step>` series (see
[`context.md`](context.md) for the index). `06_the_logger` is the direct
predecessor (not yet ported at the time of this writing — see
[`06_the_logger`](06_the_logger)); this step adds `Boukensha::RunDSL` and
`Boukensha.run` on top of it.

Confirmed via `diff` against `06_the_logger`: `registry.rb`, `tool.rb`,
`message.rb`, `tasks/*.rb`, `backends/*.rb`, `client.rb`, `prompt_builder.rb`
are **byte-identical**. `context.rb` only changes cosmetically
(ivar-alignment whitespace, and loses its trailing newline). `config.rb`
re-adds the `mud_host`/`mud_port`/`mud_username`/`mud_password` accessors
that `06_the_logger` had just removed (see that step's plan — a short-lived
Ruby-side deletion, now undone) plus a cosmetic `if`/`end` reformat of
`load_env`. `errors.rb` re-adds `LoopError` (same story — still unused
anywhere in this step; see behavioural note below). `logger.rb` gains one
new method (`turn`) and a `subscribe` hook. Everything else listed below is
new.

